gllvmTMB is EXPERIMENTAL (lifecycle: experimental). Use at your own risk: the package is not complete, is not fully human-verified, and needs extensive further validation. Point estimates are the primary inferential output, but evidence is route- and regime-specific. Broad interval coverage is not certified. One narrow two-sided Gaussian total-variance profile regime has a documented 0.94 floor. See the Current limitations and boundaries page for scope.
======================================================================
ROUTE A: phylo_indep(1 + x | species, tree = tree)
======================================================================
n_sp = 60, n_traits = 3, n_rep = 6, n_obs = 1080
formula: value ~ 0 + trait + phylo_indep(1 + x | species, tree = tree_A)
family: gaussian(); control: gllvmTMBcontrol(se = TRUE)

Fit time: 2.1 s | opt$convergence: 0 | pd_hessian: TRUE

---- Route A (phylo_indep, tree in-keyword): sd_report$par.fixed block-name table ----

         b_fix  log_sigma_eps theta_dep_chol 
             3              1              9 
  pdHess: TRUE

Route A slope-variance parameter is theta_dep_chol (per
tests/testthat/test-phylo-indep-slope-gaussian.R: phylo_indep desugars to
phylo_slope's block-diagonal Cholesky engine, NOT theta_diag_B_slope /
theta_rr_B_slope).

CORRECTED PACKING (an earlier version of this script had this wrong --
see the results file's provenance note). Per src/gllvmTMB.cpp:1909-1935
and R/lambda-constraint.R's dep_chol_crossblock_pins(), theta_dep_chol
packs ALL C diagonal entries FIRST (log-scale), THEN the strictly-lower
triangle in column-major order (raw/unconstrained scale). After the
block-diagonal cross-block pins (Design 79/80), the 3*n_traits FREE
entries are, in order: the 2*n_traits diagonals interleaved
(int_1, slope_1, int_2, slope_2, ...), THEN the n_traits within-block
off-diagonal entries (L21 per trait, raw scale) -- NOT 3-entry
(int, slope, offdiag) blocks per trait as first assumed.

Consequently the within-block Cholesky factor for trait t is
  L_t = [[L11, 0], [L21, L22]],  L11 = exp(diag_int), L22 = exp(diag_slope)
and Sigma_t = L_t %*% t(L_t) gives:
  Var(intercept_t) = L11^2                    -- univariate log-SD, exp() suffices
  Var(slope_t)      = L21^2 + L22^2            -- NOT exp(diag_slope)^2 alone
So the slope variance depends on TWO parameters (diag_slope AND the raw
off-diagonal L21) whenever the within-trait intercept-slope correlation is
nonzero, and needs a MULTIVARIATE delta method / numerical Jacobian using
the (diag_slope, L21) 2x2 cov.fixed submatrix -- the same treatment
Route B's theta_rr_B_slope needed, not the univariate exp() shortcut.

